    {
      "question": "Does the flux-scale offset belong to Rubin or to the reference?",
      "verdict": "Rubin side of the comparison, or the aperture method common to all pairings",
      "basis": "Paired median log-scale differences between reference pairings: legacy-vs-des: -0.0016 dex [-0.0046, +0.0005] over 71 regions. 2 independently calibrated references, reduced by different pipelines, agreeing about Rubin cannot all be wrong in the same direction by chance.",
      "cannotDistinguish": "This does not separate a Rubin zeropoint from a systematic in the 1.5 arcsec aperture photometry, which is identical in every pairing.",
      "referencesCompared": 2,
      "pairingsAgreeing": [
        "legacy-vs-des"
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "Does the density-dependent flux scale belong to Rubin or to the reference?",
      "verdict": "common to every pairing, therefore Rubin's or the aperture method's",
      "basis": "Scale against matched-source count: legacy -0.334 (p 0.0009, n 91), des -0.269 (p 0.0010, n 140). Every pairing shows it. The references are independently calibrated and were reduced by different pipelines, so what the comparisons share is Rubin and the 1.5 arcsec aperture photometry applied identically to all of them.",
      "direction": "The scale falls as matched-source count rises: Rubin measures relatively less flux in the aperture where sources are denser, which is the sign expected if neighbouring flux is handled differently by the two sides.",
